From w_rolke at rumac.upr.clu.edu  Fri Nov 27 15:47:41 1998
From: w_rolke at rumac.upr.clu.edu (Wolfgang Rolke)
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 1998 07:47:41 -0800
Subject: [S] Replies I got for my posting: Bandwidths in two dimensions
Message-ID: <365EC99C.B7F4738F@rumac.upr.clu.edu>

About two weeks ago I sent the following posting to s-news:

> I am looking for a method to do bandwidth selection for kernel
> density estimators for bivariate data, maybe something analogous to
> cross-validation. Also, does somebody already have a function written
> that does this? A function written in either Splus or in Fortran would

> be perfect. By the way, my data is highly non-normal, with multiple
peaks,
> and I will need to use two bandwidths for the x and the y axis.

Several people have asked me to summarize the responses to s-news, so
here
it is:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Andreas Krause <Andreas.Krause at genedata.com> wrote

Hi,
did you take a look at Wolfgang Haerdle's book(s)?
The code for one of the books is available from statlib (haerdle
library).
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

"sinclair, andrew (MIP London)" <asinclair at edfman.co.uk> wrote:

I would be very interested to hear any answers you may
get.

BTW it is possible you might be able rescale the axes
and get away with one bandwidth.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ashish Sharma <a.sharma at unsw.edu.au> wrote

I have codes for estimating a single bandwidth to be used to measure the
full
variability of a multivariate sample. The way this works is that the
bandwidth
in all coordinate directions is assumed to be the same single bandwidth
multiplied by a factor related to the standard deviation of the data. I
use an
optimization routine to find the optimal bandwidth, using LSCV as my
criterion
for when the bandwidth is "optimal".

I would be interested in replies others send you directly which have
optimization routines set up to optimize the bandwidths in each
coordinate
direction using some computationally efficient method. If you need the
code I
am referring to above, please let me know. It uses a fortran code which
is
dynamically linked to the main Splus code.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Prof Brian Ripley <ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk> wrote

You need three parameters not two: why should your kernel's contours
be axis aligned?
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Anne York <york at orca.akctr.noaa.gov> wrote

 See Terrell and Scott, JASA 1985 pp 209-214 to do
bandwidth selection for data in several dimensions.

This is easy to program in Splus. I'm not sure how "in vogue"
this method is. There are no doubt a lot of different viewpoints
on this one.  Unfortunately, I wrote my function assuming that
the dimension is 1, but it is easy to fix. If you want the
function, I'll be happy to send it to you.
The reference to the Splus program I sent you should be:
Terrell 1990, JASA, The maximal smoothing principle in density
estimation. -- not Terrell and Scott as I originally wrote.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Well, this is it. Unfortunately nobody had an answer to my question,
namely
a method for computing the two (or maybe three ?) bandwidths
needed for a bivariate nonparametric density estimate. Maybe somebody
who missed the last posting would like to give it a try this time?

Wolfgang

